1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,930
Hello everyone, this is Ria!

2
00:00:03,930 --> 00:00:10,270
Have you ever been to Korea?

3
00:00:10,270 --> 00:00:12,380
Have you been?

4
00:00:12,380 --> 00:00:18,590
Or are you thinking about traveling there someday?

5
00:00:18,590 --> 00:00:21,250
Are you planning to travel?

6
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:27,590
Today, I’m going to talk about important Korean culture.

7
00:00:27,590 --> 00:00:35,490
I’ll tell you about Korean culture that you absolutely need to know.

8
00:00:35,490 --> 00:00:42,480
I’ll also talk about things you shouldn’t do in Korea.

9
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:50,280
First, in Korea, there is a culture of respecting elders.

10
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:57,600
As you probably already know, the Korean language has honorifics.

11
00:00:57,600 --> 00:00:59,160
Honorifics

12
00:00:59,160 --> 00:01:10,500
So, when speaking to older people or strangers, you always use honorific language.

13
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:14,170
You always use honorifics.

14
00:01:14,170 --> 00:01:22,620
And not just words, you also need to be careful with your actions.

15
00:01:22,620 --> 00:01:24,900
Actions

16
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:28,130
You have to be careful with your actions too.

17
00:01:28,130 --> 00:01:35,380
Especially when giving or receiving something.

18
00:01:35,380 --> 00:01:37,790
When giving or receiving

19
00:01:37,790 --> 00:01:48,420
If you just hand something over with one hand like this, it can feel a bit rude.

20
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:52,190
It can feel rude.

21
00:01:52,190 --> 00:01:54,880
Rude

22
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:57,760
You receive with two hands.

23
00:01:57,760 --> 00:01:59,170
Two hands

24
00:01:59,170 --> 00:02:05,630
Or usually you support it with your other hand when giving or receiving.

25
00:02:05,630 --> 00:02:07,710
Support it when giving.

26
00:02:07,710 --> 00:02:09,310
Pen

27
00:02:09,310 --> 00:02:11,690
“Here’s the pen.”

28
00:02:11,690 --> 00:02:16,700
“Here’s the card.” You support it like this when giving.

29
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:17,910
Here you go

30
00:02:17,910 --> 00:02:19,080
Like this

31
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:29,060
But if a supermarket or restaurant is very busy, sometimes people give or receive with one hand.

32
00:02:29,060 --> 00:02:30,450
Sometimes

33
00:02:30,450 --> 00:02:35,710
But most of the time, people support with their hand.

34
00:02:35,710 --> 00:02:43,930
This might feel a little difficult or awkward at first.

35
00:02:43,930 --> 00:02:48,740
I once watched an American TV show.

36
00:02:48,740 --> 00:02:50,080
TV show

37
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:55,070
“TV show” is a shortened word for drama.

38
00:02:55,070 --> 00:02:56,450
Shortened word

39
00:02:56,450 --> 00:03:05,170
I watched a very famous show called “Young Sheldon.”

40
00:03:05,170 --> 00:03:09,460
Ah, wait a minute... Is this really a TV show?

41
00:03:09,460 --> 00:03:11,180
It is, right?

42
00:03:11,180 --> 00:03:13,500
It’s an American drama, right?

43
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:22,460
Sometimes I get confused whether a show is an American drama or a British drama.

44
00:03:22,460 --> 00:03:26,940
So what is a British drama, everyone?

45
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:30,830
British dramas are called “Young dramas.”

46
00:03:30,830 --> 00:03:36,680
British dramas are shortened to “Young dramas.”

47
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:39,570
British drama, Young drama

48
00:03:39,570 --> 00:03:46,430
Then what about Chinese dramas in Korean?

49
00:03:46,430 --> 00:03:49,310
They’re called “Jung dramas.” Jung drama.

50
00:03:49,310 --> 00:03:53,820
What about Japanese dramas? What do you think?

51
00:03:53,820 --> 00:03:56,790
Try to guess now.

52
00:03:56,790 --> 00:04:00,130
Yes, they’re called “Il dramas.” Il drama.

53
00:04:00,130 --> 00:04:09,610
Anyway, I was really shocked when I watched the show “Young Sheldon.”

54
00:04:09,610 --> 00:04:12,210
Really a culture shock

55
00:04:12,210 --> 00:04:13,880
Culture shock

56
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:19,300
The main character is a very young kid.

57
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:20,600
Young kid

58
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:28,390
That young main character and his friend went somewhere.

59
00:04:28,390 --> 00:04:33,000
I don’t remember exactly where.

60
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:39,620
But it was a place with a lot of adults.

61
00:04:39,620 --> 00:04:40,940
Lecture

62
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:44,610
It was a place where a lecture was being held.

63
00:04:44,610 --> 00:04:52,690
But the kid crossed his legs while listening to the lecture.

64
00:04:52,690 --> 00:04:55,190
Crossed his legs.

65
00:04:55,190 --> 00:04:59,900
Watching that made me very uncomfortable.

66
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:03,360
Very uncomfortable.

67
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:06,070
“No, please don’t do that.”

68
00:05:06,070 --> 00:05:10,940
I felt really uncomfortable like that.

69
00:05:10,940 --> 00:05:18,160
Even though this drama isn’t set in Korea, it just felt uncomfortable.

70
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:24,590
I guess I’m Korean to the bone.

71
00:05:24,590 --> 00:05:27,490
Korean to the bone

72
00:05:27,490 --> 00:05:34,660
In Korea, you don’t cross your legs in front of elders.

73
00:05:34,660 --> 00:05:37,890
You never do that.

74
00:05:37,890 --> 00:05:47,970
If I were listening to a lecture and crossed my legs? That just doesn’t make sense...

75
00:05:47,970 --> 00:05:55,710
But in this drama, the kids are kind of geniuses.

76
00:05:55,710 --> 00:05:56,790
Genius

77
00:05:56,790 --> 00:06:01,140
So they have a bit of an arrogant personality.

78
00:06:01,140 --> 00:06:03,220
Arrogant personality

79
00:06:03,220 --> 00:06:04,960
Arrogant

80
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:08,480
Is that why they crossed their legs?

81
00:06:08,480 --> 00:06:16,180
Or I wonder if this is actually acceptable in other countries.

82
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:17,560
I’m curious.

83
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:20,740
How about in your countries?

84
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:35,330
Is it okay to cross your legs in front of other adults, not your really close family or parents?

85
00:06:35,330 --> 00:06:40,630
Also, I traveled to Japan.

86
00:06:40,630 --> 00:06:45,010
And I noticed a cultural difference.

87
00:06:45,010 --> 00:06:46,430
Cultural difference

88
00:06:46,430 --> 00:06:47,970
Subway

89
00:06:47,970 --> 00:06:52,930
I noticed a cultural difference on the subway.

90
00:06:52,930 --> 00:06:59,520
On the subway, there are seats called “priority seats.”

91
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:01,450
Priority seats

92
00:07:01,450 --> 00:07:13,300
These seats are for elderly people, grandmothers, grandfathers, or people with disabilities.

93
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:21,060
Usually there are regular seats and next to them are the priority seats.

94
00:07:21,060 --> 00:07:22,820
They are separate.

95
00:07:22,820 --> 00:07:25,330
But here’s the interesting thing.

96
00:07:25,330 --> 00:07:33,900
In Korea, even if those seats are empty, young people don’t sit there.

97
00:07:33,900 --> 00:07:42,650
Even if there are no elderly people around, those seats are never taken.

98
00:07:42,650 --> 00:07:43,980
They leave them empty.

99
00:07:43,980 --> 00:07:49,310
But in Japan, people just sit there.

100
00:07:49,310 --> 00:07:51,460
They just sit.

101
00:07:51,460 --> 00:07:54,310
They just sit there.

102
00:07:54,310 --> 00:08:02,780
Not only in Japan, but everywhere I traveled, it was the same.

103
00:08:02,780 --> 00:08:04,670
It was the same.

104
00:08:04,670 --> 00:08:11,260
In Singapore, people also just sat in the priority seats.

105
00:08:11,260 --> 00:08:19,210
Everyone sat there without any problem.

106
00:08:19,210 --> 00:08:23,460
I found that really interesting.

107
00:08:23,460 --> 00:08:25,120
It was interesting.

108
00:08:25,120 --> 00:08:34,720
They just sit there, and if a person with a disability comes, they give up the seat then.

109
00:08:34,720 --> 00:08:37,180
They give up the seat then.

110
00:08:37,180 --> 00:08:45,280
Honestly, I thought it would be the same since these are all Asian countries.

111
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:49,780
But Korea is the strictest.

112
00:08:49,780 --> 00:08:51,760
The strictest.

113
00:08:51,760 --> 00:08:55,190
They are very strict about priority seats.

114
00:08:55,190 --> 00:09:02,390
I haven’t seen any place as strict about priority seats as Korea.

115
00:09:02,390 --> 00:09:10,060
So if you come to Korea and sit in a priority seat...

116
00:09:10,060 --> 00:09:18,450
People might look at you like, “Huh? What’s going on? What’s that?”

117
00:09:18,450 --> 00:09:23,320
Please remember my podcast then.

118
00:09:23,320 --> 00:09:28,670
Also, in Korea, there is almost no tipping culture.

119
00:09:28,670 --> 00:09:30,130
Tipping culture

120
00:09:30,130 --> 00:09:36,430
When you think of tipping culture, the country that comes to mind is the United States.

121
00:09:36,430 --> 00:09:42,910
In countries like the U.S., you give tips at restaurants.

122
00:09:42,910 --> 00:09:47,320
But in Korea, you don’t give tips.

123
00:09:47,320 --> 00:09:55,100
So when you pay, you just pay the exact price.

124
00:09:55,100 --> 00:10:01,410
If you give a tip, the staff might be surprised.

125
00:10:01,410 --> 00:10:03,680
They might be surprised.

126
00:10:03,680 --> 00:10:06,540
They might be shocked.

127
00:10:06,540 --> 00:10:13,440
I really like that Korea doesn’t have a tipping culture.

128
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:16,130
I really like it.

129
00:10:16,130 --> 00:10:18,270
It’s really convenient.

130
00:10:18,270 --> 00:10:28,610
But once, a famous bakery made a tip box. A tip box!

131
00:10:28,610 --> 00:10:34,500
At that time, the Korean internet went crazy.

132
00:10:34,500 --> 00:10:36,320
It was crazy.

133
00:10:36,320 --> 00:10:42,430
Are you kidding? Tips in Korea? What is this nonsense?

134
00:10:42,430 --> 00:10:48,510
People got really angry.

135
00:10:48,510 --> 00:10:59,590
Because if a famous place starts accepting tips, other places will follow.

136
00:10:59,590 --> 00:11:01,000
They will follow.

137
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:05,950
They will start following.

138
00:11:05,950 --> 00:11:15,520
So people got very angry, and eventually, the tip box disappeared.

139
00:11:15,520 --> 00:11:17,380
It disappeared.

140
00:11:17,380 --> 00:11:30,480
Anyway, if tourists start giving tips one by one, tip boxes will appear and other places will follow.

141
00:11:30,480 --> 00:11:36,590
So in Korea, you don’t tip at restaurants.

142
00:11:36,590 --> 00:11:44,460
Today, I talked about important cultural things in Korea.

143
00:11:44,460 --> 00:11:50,830
I talked about culture you must follow in Korea.

144
00:11:50,830 --> 00:11:56,450
Actually, it’s okay if you make mistakes in Korea.

145
00:11:56,450 --> 00:12:01,240
It’s okay to make mistakes!

146
00:12:01,240 --> 00:12:13,040
I think the most important thing is to try to understand the culture and have a fun time. Right?

147
00:12:13,040 --> 00:12:24,260
Actually, besides what I talked about today, there are many other important cultural things.

148
00:12:24,260 --> 00:12:30,350
Someday, I’ll come back with a part two.

149
00:12:30,350 --> 00:12:37,310
Did you find any culture interesting while traveling in Korea?

150
00:12:37,310 --> 00:12:42,270
Please be sure to tell me your stories in the comments!

151
00:12:42,270 --> 00:12:48,440
I’ll include those comments in part two. Okay?

152
00:12:48,440 --> 00:12:55,200
See you next time with a more fun podcast! Bye :)
